A device callback can take its samples in double precision

AudioIODeviceCallback is given floats. AudioProcessorPlayer plays a processor that processes in double precision through a copy -- the device's floats copied into a buffer of doubles, processed, and copied back -- so everything such a processor computes is rounded to a float before the device writes it, even when the device writes 32-bit integers or 64-bit floats, and what the device reads reaches the processor through a float as well.

AudioIODeviceCallback gets wantsDoublePrecision(), false unless it is overridden, and audioDeviceIOCallbackWithContextDouble(), the same callback with doubles, which writes silence unless it is overridden. A device that can convert between its own format and doubles asks the callback before each block, since the answer can change while the device runs -- a player's changes with its processor's precision -- and while it is yes, converts to and from doubles and calls the double method. A device that cannot keeps calling the float method, and a callback that overrides neither method is never called with doubles, so nothing changes for either.

AudioDeviceManager wants doubles when every callback it has wants them, asked under the lock its callbacks are called under, and then adds the callbacks' outputs together in double precision, its level meters reading the doubles. A callback added between the question and the block it answers is called with doubles for that block, and one that does not want them writes silence, the default. CallbackMaxSizeEnforcer passes the question and the double blocks through. AudioProcessorPlayer wants doubles while its processor processes in double precision, and processes each block in the precision the device gives it: straight through when that is the processor's own, and through a copy in the processor's precision when it is not, as it always did for doubles. StandalonePluginHolder passes both on to its player, and mutes the input in either precision.

The double method has a name of its own rather than overloading audioDeviceIOCallbackWithContext(), so that the callbacks that override the float method alone -- in JUCE, and in the code of those who use it -- do not hide it and draw -Woverloaded-virtual.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

